    "title": "Sports News Roundup",
    "text": "• The Milwaukee Brewers swept the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NLCS to reach the World Series for the first time since 1982.\n• Petey Halpin hit a go-ahead, two-run homer, and Gavin Williams struck out nine over five innings as the Cleveland Guardians beat the Tampa Bay Rays 3-2 in Game 3 to take a 2-1 lead in the AL Championship Series.\n• Ja’Quan McMillian intercepted Sam Darnold’s pass in overtime, and Wil Lutz kicked a 53-yard field goal that gave the Denver Broncos a 17-14 victory over the Seattle Seahawks.\n• The Philadelphia Eagles have ruled out quarterback Jalen Hurts for Sunday's game against Carolina because of a superficial blood clot.\n• Shohei Ohtani hit a three-run homer for the Los Angeles Dodgers against the Milwaukee Brewers in Game 4 of the National League Championship Series.\n• Green Bay Packers running back Josh Jacobs has filed a lawsuit in an attempt to have evidence in his battery case permanently sealed.\n• The NBA season will begin with 6 coaches in new places, including 3 first-time sideline bosses.\n• Jon Rahm officially can leave LIV Golf and possibly pursue a return to the PGA Tour under a separation agreement.\n• Brady Tkachuk has given Florida a thrilling overtime win against the Vancouver Canucks, scoring his second consecutive hat trick.\n• Gianni Infantino has given an apology to his toughest critics at a showdown meeting in Zurich but no new details or documents on FIFA issues that most concern them.",
    "summary": "Shohei Ohtani's early home run helped put the Dodgers ahead 4-0, but the Brewers rallied to complete a four-game sweep of the two-time defending World Series champions. Halpin’s go-ahead homer flips Game 3 as Guardians beat Rays 3-2 for 2-1 AL Championship Series lead CLEVELAND (AP) — Petey Halpin hit a go-ahead, two-run homer, Gavin Williams struck out nine over five innings and the Cleveland Guardians beat the Tampa Bay Rays 3-2 in Game 3 to take a 2-1 lead in the AL Championship Series.",
